---
import Icon from '@/components/Icon.astro';
---

<div class="panel">
  <h3><Icon name="box" /> What is a VRF?</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Virtual Routing and Forwarding (VRF)</strong> lets a single router hold multiple completely
    separate routing tables at the same time. Think of it as running several independent routers inside
    one physical box.
  </p>
  <ul class="mt-[6px] ml-[20px] list-disc leading-[1.9]">
    <li>Each VRF has its own interfaces, routing table, and default gateway.</li>
    <li>
      The same IP network (e.g. <code>10.0.0.0/24</code>) can be reused in different VRFs without
      conflict.
    </li>
    <li>
      Traffic in one VRF stays completely isolated from other VRFs unless you explicitly leak routes
      between them.
    </li>
    <li>
      Used without MPLS this is called <strong>VRF-Lite</strong> &mdash; very common on Cisco branch and
      campus routers.
    </li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="panel">
  <h3><Icon name="link" /> VRF vs VLAN</h3>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="ref-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>VLAN</th><th>VRF</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr
          ><td><strong>OSI Layer</strong></td><td>Layer 2 &mdash; switch / broadcast domain</td><td
            >Layer 3 &mdash; IP routing table instance</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><strong>What it separates</strong></td><td>MAC broadcasts and switch ports</td><td
            >IP routing, subnets, and default routes</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><strong>Typical example</strong></td><td>VLAN 10 Users, VLAN 20 Voice</td><td
            >VRF PROD, VRF MGMT, VRF GUEST</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><strong>Used together?</strong></td><td colspan="2"
            >Yes &mdash; the SVI for VLAN 10 can live inside VRF PROD so users in VLAN 10 use the
            PROD routing table and default gateway.</td
          ></tr
        >
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="tip mt-[10px]">
    For VLAN config use the <strong>VLAN Config Generator</strong>. For WAN/VPN background see <strong
      >WAN &amp; VPN Services</strong
    > and <strong>Network Types</strong> in this toolkit.
  </div>
</div>

<div class="panel">
  <h3><Icon name="bullseye" /> Common VRF Use Cases</h3>
  <div class="result-grid mt-[8px]">
    <div class="result-item">
      <div class="ri-label">VRF-Lite on Campus</div><div
        class="ri-value font-sans text-[11.5px] text-text2"
      >
        Split management, users, and guest into separate VRFs on the same distribution router or
        firewall.
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="result-item">
      <div class="ri-label">WAN / DMVPN Segmentation</div><div
        class="ri-value font-sans text-[11.5px] text-text2"
      >
        Run a DMVPN tunnel per VRF so production and management traffic stay separate over the same
        physical WAN.
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="result-item">
      <div class="ri-label">Per-Customer VPN</div><div
        class="ri-value font-sans text-[11.5px] text-text2"
      >
        Each customer gets its own VRF &mdash; overlapping RFC1918 addresses are no problem because
        each VRF is isolated.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="panel">
  <h3><Icon name="clipboard-list" /> Show &amp; Verify Commands</h3>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="ref-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="mono">show vrf</td><td>List all VRFs, interfaces assigned, and state</td></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td class="mono">show vrf detail</td><td
            >Full VRF details including RD, RT, and interfaces</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td class="mono">show ip route vrf NAME</td><td>Routing table for a specific VRF</td></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td class="mono">show ip interface brief</td><td
            >Verify interface VRF assignment and IP</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td class="mono">ping vrf NAME x.x.x.x</td><td
            >Ping sourced from inside a specific VRF</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td class="mono">traceroute vrf NAME x.x.x.x</td><td>Traceroute inside a specific VRF</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td class="mono">show ip eigrp vrf NAME neighbors</td><td
            >EIGRP neighbors inside a VRF</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td class="mono">show ip bgp vpnv4 vrf NAME</td><td
            >BGP prefixes for a specific VRF (MPLS/VPNv4)</td
          ></tr
        >
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</div>
